          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            %em zeta function
            originates in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+theory"} number theory
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ζ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \zeta
            \-functions are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meromorphic+functions"} meromorphic functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi ζ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \mapsto \zeta(s)
            on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+plane"} complex plane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+continuations"} analytic continuations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traces"} traces
            of powers
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi Tr
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mi H
                      %mo )
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  s \mapsto Tr \left(\frac{1}{H}\right)^s
          %p
            of suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+differential+operators"} elliptic differential operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            (in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            these are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regularization+%28physics%29"} regularized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traces"} traces
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagators"} Feynman propagators
            leading to expressions for
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+amplitudes"} vacuum amplitudes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            these are analytic continuations in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            of sums of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ↦
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi λ
                  %msup
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi s
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  s \mapsto \underset{\lambda}{\sum}  \lambda^{-s}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the summation is over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenvalues"} eigenvalues
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
          %p
            Indeed, such
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+functions+of+elliptic+differential+operators"} zeta functions of elliptic differential operators
            constitutes one class of examples of zeta functions. Of particular interest is the case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laplace+operator"} Laplace operator
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperbolic+manifold"} hyperbolic manifold
            and in particular on a hyperbolic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function+of+a+Riemann+surface"} zeta function of a Riemann surface
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Selberg+zeta+function"} Selberg zeta function
          %p
            In modern language one also speaks of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-functions"} L-functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            of
            %em the
            canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laplace+operator"} Laplace operator
            of that space, an L-function is defined from an extra “twisting” information such as that of a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+bundle"} flat bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+system+of+coefficients"} local system of coefficients
            on the space (and is hence like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            of the corresponding twisted/coupled
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laplace+operator"} Laplace operator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-functions"} L-functions
            are: these are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meromorphic+functions"} meromorphic functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \mapsto L(s)
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+plane"} complex plane
            such that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℜ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(s) \gt 1
                they have a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} converging
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/series"} series
                expansion of the above form, and/or a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+product"} multiplicative series
                expression, the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+product"} Euler product
            %li
              %p
                such that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+continuation"} analytic continuation
                of the series expression exists to a meromorphic function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(-)
                on the complex plane;
            %li
              %p
                and such that the result satisfies a
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+equation"} functional equation
                which says that the product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat L
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                with some correcion functions satisfies
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi L
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo −
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mover
                          %mi L
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat L(1-s) = \hat L(s)
          %p
            Proceeding from the above class of examples in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analytic+geometry"} complex analytic geometry
            one may wonder if there are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogy"} analogs
            also in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+field+analogy"} function field analogy
            there are. All the way down “on
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spec%28Z%29"} Spec(Z)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Selberg+zeta+function"} Selberg zeta function
            is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+zeta+function"} Riemann zeta function
            %em historically
            is the first of all zeta functions, defined by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+continuation"} analytic continuation
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/series"} series
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ↦
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mn ∞
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi s
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  s \mapsto \underoverset{n = 1}{\infty}{\sum} n^{-s}
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+hypothesis"} Riemann hypothesis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjecture"} conjectures
            a characterization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/roots"} roots
            of this zeta function and is regarded as one of the outstanding problems in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
          %p
            More generally, over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+curves"} arithmetic curves
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+of+a+commutative+ring"} spectra
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings+of+integers"} rings of integers
            of more general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+fields"} number fields
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+L-functions"} Artin L-functions
            defined intrinsically in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+polynomials"} characteristic polynomials
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+representations"} Galois representations
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+reciprocity"} Artin reciprocity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirichlet+L-functions"} Dirichlet L-functions
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hecke+L-functions"} Hecke L-functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+correspondence"} Langlands correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjecture"} conjecture
            asserts that the Artin L-function may be expressed “arithmetically” as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphic+L-function"} automorphic L-function
            of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphic+form"} automorphic form
          %p
            Similarly on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+curves"} arithmetic curves
            given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+fields"} function fields
            there is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goss+zeta+function"} Goss zeta function
            and in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+arithmetic+geometry"} higher dimensional arithmetic geometry
            the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+zeta+function"} Weil zeta function
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+conjectures"} Weil conjectures
          %p
            When interpreting the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+morphisms"} Frobenius morphisms
            that appear in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+L-functions"} Artin L-functions
            geometrically as flows (as discussed at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Borger's+absolute+geometry#Motivation"} Borger’s arithmetic geometry – Motivation
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function+of+a+dynamical+system"} zeta function of a dynamical system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+polynomials"} Alexander polynomials
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+theory"} knot theory
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+topology"} arithmetic topology
